## Invention Overview: Intelligent Crustacean Portioning Apparatus

The invention relates to an automated apparatus for receiving, identifying, securing, portioning, and discharging crustaceans, including lobsters, crabs, crayfish, prawns, and similar shell-bearing seafood. The apparatus combines machine vision, sensor-based positioning, adaptive fixturing, and programmable butchering tools to produce consistent portions while reducing manual handling, product damage, and processing waste.

### 1. Intake Component

An intake assembly receives individual crustaceans from a hopper, conveyor, chute, or manual loading station. The intake assembly may include:

- A singulation mechanism for separating overlapping animals;
- A metering conveyor that advances one crustacean at a time;
- Guides or compliant paddles for establishing a preliminary orientation;
- Presence, weight, and size sensors; and
- A reject or recirculation path for improperly loaded products.

The intake surfaces may be formed from food-safe, corrosion-resistant materials and configured for washdown and sanitation.

### 2. Sensing and Positioning Component

A sensing station characterizes each crustacean and determines its pose relative to the processing equipment. The sensing system may employ one or more color cameras, depth cameras, structured-light scanners, X-ray sensors, ultrasound sensors, hyperspectral sensors, force sensors, or combinations thereof.

A controller processes the sensor data to identify anatomical features such as:

- Carapace boundaries;
- Head, thorax, abdomen, and tail regions;
- Claws, legs, and joints;
- Dorsal and ventral surfaces;
- Shell seams and articulation points;
- Estimated meat-bearing regions; and
- Damaged, missing, or abnormal structures.

The controller may generate a three-dimensional product model and calculate a target orientation and individualized cutting plan. A positioning mechanism, such as a robotic gripper, rotating cradle, belt array, or articulated guide system, then translates and rotates the crustacean into a selected processing pose.

### 3. Fixed Holding Component

After positioning, the crustacean is transferred into a fixed holding assembly that constrains movement during portioning. The holding assembly may include independently controlled clamps, contoured supports, vacuum elements, compliant fingers, pins, or shell-engaging jaws.

The holder can adjust to variations in species, size, shell hardness, and anatomical asymmetry. Force or pressure feedback allows the controller to apply sufficient retention force without excessively crushing the shell or damaging recoverable meat. Interlocks may confirm that the product is properly seated and that tools cannot operate unless the holding area is secured.

In some embodiments, separate holding elements immobilize the body, tail, claws, or appendages so that one or more portions can be removed in a controlled sequence.

### 4. Adaptive Butchering Component

An adaptive butchering assembly performs cuts, separations, cracking, splitting, trimming, or meat extraction according to the individualized processing plan. The assembly may include one or more:

- Rotary or reciprocating blades;
- Guillotine cutters;
- Water-jet or abrasive-jet cutters;
- Ultrasonic knives;
- Shears or joint-separation tools;
- Shell cracking tools;
- Drills, punches, or coring devices; and
- Robotic cutting heads having multiple degrees of freedom.

The controller adjusts tool path, cutting depth, angle, speed, and applied force based on the sensed anatomy and real-time tool feedback. Force, torque, vibration, acoustic, optical, or current sensors may detect shell penetration, joint separation, blade deflection, or contact with an unintended structure. The tool path can consequently be modified during operation to compensate for product movement, shell thickness, anatomical variation, or tool wear.

Selectable processing programs may produce, for example, separated claws, leg clusters, split bodies, tail portions, medallions, half-shell products, or consumer-ready serving portions. Portion targets may be based on geometry, weight, expected meat yield, customer specification, or downstream packaging requirements.

### 5. Discharge Component

A discharge assembly removes processed portions and directs them to selected output destinations. The assembly may include conveyors, chutes, robotic pickers, gates, sorting bins, or weighing stations. Portions may be classified and routed according to:

- Portion type;
- Weight or size grade;
- Quality classification;
- Detected damage;
- Meat-yield estimate; or
- Packaging or cooking destination.

Shell fragments, undersized material, and nonconforming products may be separately discharged for recovery, reprocessing, or disposal. Sensors can verify that the holder and cutting zone have been cleared before the next crustacean is introduced.

### Control and Intelligence

A central control system coordinates intake, sensing, positioning, holding, cutting, and discharge. The system may use rule-based algorithms, trained machine-learning models, or both to recognize anatomical landmarks and optimize portioning decisions. Processing results—including cut accuracy, measured weight, tool forces, yield, and quality inspection data—may be recorded and used to refine later cutting plans.

The controller may also support:

- Automatic species and size recognition;
- Recipe selection based on production orders;
- Traceability of individual products or batches;
- Predictive maintenance and blade-wear monitoring;
- Sanitation-cycle management;
- Remote diagnostics; and
- Integration with packaging, inventory, and plant-control systems.

### Representative Operating Sequence

1. A crustacean is received and singulated by the intake component.  
2. Sensors acquire dimensional and anatomical data.  
3. The controller identifies the product and generates a processing plan.  
4. The positioning mechanism places the crustacean into the required pose.  
5. The fixed holding assembly secures selected anatomical regions.  
6. The adaptive butchering assembly executes and, when necessary, corrects the planned cuts.  
7. Processed portions are released, inspected, sorted, and discharged.  
8. Waste is removed, the station is checked or cleaned, and the cycle repeats.

### Principal Advantages

The apparatus accommodates natural variation among crustaceans rather than relying solely on fixed cutting locations. Potential advantages include improved portion consistency, increased recoverable meat yield, reduced shell fragmentation, lower labor requirements, enhanced operator safety, better traceability, and rapid changeover between species, sizes, and desired product configurations. Modular construction further allows the intake, sensing, holding, butchering, and discharge components to be independently serviced, sanitized, or adapted to different processing environments.
